# Isolation map — worktrees, owners, and the collision

One copy. Every `TASK.md` references this file for its branch and its wave; if a task and this
file disagree, **this file wins and one of them needs fixing.**

Phase 7 says the separate workspaces are the *easy* half. This file exists for the hard half.

---

## 1 · The waves

Tasks in the same wave run concurrently. A wave closes when every task in it is **verified**, not
when every task reports done — a claim is not evidence.

| Wave | Tasks | Gates on | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| W1 | `<ids>` | — | `<why these are first>` |
| W2 | `<ids>` | W1 | |
| W3 | `<ids>` | W2 | |

State the gating relation, not just the order. "W2 after W1" is a schedule; "W2 gates on W1
because it consumes the schema W1 lands" is a reason, and a reason survives a change of plan.

---

## 2 · Worktrees

Each task gets its own checkout. **The deployed or live checkout is never a workspace** — read
from it if you must, write to it never.

| Task | Branch | Worktree path |
|---|---|---|
| `<id>` | `<prefix>/<id>-<slug>` | `<path>` |

Creating one:

```
git worktree add <path> -b <prefix>/<id>-<slug> <base-ref>
```

Removing one, after its branch is merged and its findings are collected:

```
git worktree remove <path>
```

Do not remove a worktree you did not create, and do not remove one whose task is not verified —
uncommitted work inside it is invisible from outside.

---

## 3 · The contended-file list — the point of this document

**Which files do many tasks want?** Every project has some: a build script, a schema file, a
route table, a shared client, a registry, a config. Several branches editing one file is several
conflicts resolved by whoever merges last — the same collision, relocated to merge time, where it
is most expensive and least visible.

Find them before the work starts, not at merge:

```
# rough but effective: which paths appear in more than one task's "What you own"?
grep -h -A20 'What you own' tasks/*/TASK.md | grep -oE '[a-zA-Z0-9_./-]+\.[a-z]+' | sort | uniq -d
```

Record every one here. **A contended file has exactly one owner, and it is never a task agent** —
it is the integrator.

| File | Owner | Tasks that need it | Fragment name |
|---|---|---|---|
| `<path>` | integrator | `<ids>` | `<FRAGMENT>.ext` |

### The machine-readable copy

Keep the same list in an `OWNERS` file at the repository root, one entry per line, `<path or
glob><TAB><owner>`:

```
src/api/client.js	integrator
src/routes/*.js	T14
db/schema.sql	integrator
```

That file is what [`awareness.sh`](awareness.sh.template) reads in `situational` mode, so a worker
about to edit a contended file gets told whose it is **at the moment it matters**. It is a note,
never a denial — see [`_AWARENESS.md`](_AWARENESS.md.template). Blank lines and `#` comments are
ignored.

Two lists that can disagree is a defect. If you only keep one, keep `OWNERS` and generate the
table above from it.

---

## 4 · The fragment protocol

A task that needs a change in a file it does not own **does not edit it**. It writes the exact
change into its own task folder and names it in `FINDINGS.md`:

```
tasks/<id>/<FRAGMENT>.ext
```

A fragment states **where** the change goes, **what** it is, and **why** — enough that the
integrator applies it without rereading the task. A fragment that says "add this somewhere in the
client" is not a fragment, it is a request for someone else to do the work.

---

## 5 · The integrator

One role, one job: applies fragments in merge order and **writes no feature code**. Writing
feature code is what turns an integrator into another contending author, and then this whole file
describes something that is no longer true.

Merge order is the wave order. Within a wave, order by fragment dependency where one exists, and
otherwise by task id, so the sequence is reproducible rather than whoever finished first.

After each merge the integrator runs the gates. **Never merge on green fragments alone** — a
fragment that applies cleanly is not a fragment that is correct.

---

## 6 · Do NOT

- Do NOT edit a file you do not own, even trivially, even to fix something obviously broken.
  Record it; the fragment protocol exists for exactly this.
- Do NOT create or remove worktrees from inside a task agent.
- Do NOT merge your own branch.
- Do NOT treat "no merge conflict" as "no collision" — two tasks can edit different lines of one
  file and still produce something incoherent.
- Do NOT let the `OWNERS` file drift from this table. One of them is read by a machine; the other
  is read by people; they must say the same thing.
